WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.049
Let's assume you've
had COVID-19.

00:00:03.049 --> 00:00:04.684
You might believe 
you're immune,

00:00:04.684 --> 00:00:06.686
in the clear to go about
your normal life

00:00:06.686 --> 00:00:08.354
without safety precautions.

00:00:08.354 --> 00:00:10.957
But with scattered
reports of recovered cases

00:00:10.957 --> 00:00:12.525
testing positive again,

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:14.560
a lot of people are
wondering if it's possible

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.629
to develop immunity at all.

00:00:16.629 --> 00:00:19.732
So, can you catch
COVID-19 twice?

00:00:19.732 --> 00:00:22.768
The thing is, scientists
just aren't sure,

00:00:22.768 --> 00:00:24.070
because figuring that out

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.739
is a lot more complicated
than you might think.

00:00:26.739 --> 00:00:28.508
When a pathogen
like a virus

00:00:28.508 --> 00:00:30.343
manages to get
into a human,

00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:32.278
their body will 
signal an alarm.

00:00:32.278 --> 00:00:33.679
This will start 
what's referred to

00:00:33.679 --> 00:00:35.748
as the innate
immune response

00:00:35.748 --> 00:00:37.683
and consists of
physical, chemical,

00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:40.520
and cellular defenses
against pathogens.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.656
Often enough, this works,
and the invader is killed,

00:00:43.656 --> 00:00:45.725
but sometimes you 
need a second attack.

00:00:45.725 --> 00:00:47.360
The adaptive immune 
response marshals

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.128
the special forces:

00:00:49.128 --> 00:00:50.796
B cells and T cells.

00:00:50.796 --> 00:00:52.331
B cells produce antibodies

00:00:52.331 --> 00:00:54.367
that smother the
specific pathogen

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:56.469
so it can't affect
your body's cells.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:00:58.037
Shane Crotty: So, 
basically, antibodies

00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:00.106
kill virus outside of cells;

00:01:00.106 --> 00:01:02.808
killer T cells kill 
virus inside of cells.

00:01:02.808 --> 00:01:04.911
Narrator: So, once your
body fights the pathogen,

00:01:04.911 --> 00:01:06.779
what stops you from
having to fight off

00:01:06.779 --> 00:01:09.015
the same attack
over and over?

00:01:09.015 --> 00:01:10.550
Well, your body 
has a secret weapon

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.518
to protect you against
any future attacks

00:01:12.518 --> 00:01:14.987
from the same pathogen:
memory cells.

00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:15.821
Memory cells are

00:01:15.821 --> 00:01:17.023
basically the specialized

00:01:17.023 --> 00:01:19.992
T cells and B cells that
stick around as guards.

00:01:19.992 --> 00:01:22.128
That way, if you
encounter the virus again,

00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:23.529
the army will
be readily waiting

00:01:23.529 --> 00:01:25.631
to kill that
invader instantly.

00:01:25.631 --> 00:01:26.832
This is immunity.

00:01:26.832 --> 00:01:29.569
A vaccine works on this
basis by adding dead,

00:01:29.569 --> 00:01:31.137
weakened, or fragmented parts

00:01:31.137 --> 00:01:32.838
of a pathogen to your body.

00:01:32.838 --> 00:01:34.307
Not enough to cause illness,

00:01:34.307 --> 00:01:35.308
but enough to cause

00:01:35.308 --> 00:01:37.243
your body to
produce memory cells.

00:01:37.243 --> 00:01:38.611
Crotty: Normally, when
you have an infection,

00:01:38.611 --> 00:01:40.279
it's a race between 
your immune system

00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:41.581
and the infection.

00:01:41.581 --> 00:01:43.516
But if you're vaccinated,

00:01:43.516 --> 00:01:45.384
you've already
done the race part.

00:01:45.384 --> 00:01:47.486
Your immune system
has already had time

00:01:47.486 --> 00:01:49.555
to scale up and
develop immunity.

00:01:49.555 --> 00:01:50.623
Narrator: Typically, 
you would know

00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:51.691
if you have immunity,

00:01:51.691 --> 00:01:52.892
either from a vaccine

00:01:52.892 --> 00:01:54.994
or knowing you 
previously had the illness.

00:01:54.994 --> 00:01:57.263
For example, it's relatively
straightforward to know

00:01:57.263 --> 00:01:58.931
whether you've had
chickenpox or not

00:01:58.931 --> 00:02:00.666
because the symptoms
are highly unique

00:02:00.666 --> 00:02:02.568
and very easy to spot.

00:02:02.568 --> 00:02:05.438
But let's presume you
know you've had COVID-19.

00:02:05.438 --> 00:02:08.474
You now have immunity 
and are safe, right?

00:02:08.474 --> 00:02:11.010
To test this theory,
one early study

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.913
infected monkeys with
the COVID-19 virus.

00:02:13.913 --> 00:02:15.781
They then waited till
they tested negative

00:02:15.781 --> 00:02:18.985
after the infection passed
and tried to reinfect them.

00:02:18.985 --> 00:02:21.053
When the monkeys didn't
become reinfected,

00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:23.856
researchers concluded that
after one viral attack,

00:02:23.856 --> 00:02:25.858
you would be
protected from another.

00:02:25.858 --> 00:02:28.260
But this isn't a golden ticket
to thinking you're immune,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.895
because the
length and strength

00:02:29.895 --> 00:02:32.098
of that potential
immunity are unknown.

00:02:32.098 --> 00:02:33.899
And where a disease 
lies on the spectrum

00:02:33.899 --> 00:02:35.801
is influenced
by two things,

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:38.738
memory cell death rate
and virus mutation rate.

00:02:38.738 --> 00:02:40.806
Memory cell death rate
tells you at what rate

00:02:40.806 --> 00:02:43.142
those memory cells
may be lost over time.

00:02:43.142 --> 00:02:45.544
The virus mutation rate
can tell you if the virus

00:02:45.544 --> 00:02:47.980
will mutate too quickly
for your memory cells.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.783
The more a virus mutates,
the more unrecognizable

00:02:50.783 --> 00:02:52.518
it becomes to your
memory cells.

00:02:52.518 --> 00:02:54.120
Determining where
COVID-19

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.354
falls on this timescale

00:02:55.354 --> 00:02:58.024
is a vital step in
managing its spread.

00:02:58.024 --> 00:03:00.459
One indication of how long
its immunity might last

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:02.495
is to look at 
other coronaviruses.

00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:04.196
Those who have 
contracted SARS-1

00:03:04.196 --> 00:03:05.364
have been found
to have immunity

00:03:05.364 --> 00:03:07.466
for about two 
to three years,

00:03:07.466 --> 00:03:09.168
and the same time
frame has been seen

00:03:09.168 --> 00:03:12.138
in other coronaviruses that
can cause the common cold.

00:03:12.138 --> 00:03:13.506
Yet early signs have shown

00:03:13.506 --> 00:03:16.509
that this virus tends
to mutate slowly.

00:03:16.509 --> 00:03:17.643
But there's another indicator

00:03:17.643 --> 00:03:19.345
into immunity
strength and length:

00:03:19.345 --> 00:03:20.613
the serology test.

00:03:20.613 --> 00:03:23.315
Crotty: The serology
testing is a blood test

00:03:23.315 --> 00:03:25.951
for the presence of
antibodies against

00:03:25.951 --> 00:03:28.220
that specific virus or
that specific disease.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.322
Narrator: Importantly, these
are tests that can be done

00:03:30.322 --> 00:03:32.124
after you've recovered
from symptoms.

00:03:32.124 --> 00:03:33.793
Crotty: And you don't
have to know exactly

00:03:33.793 --> 00:03:35.895
when that person
was infected.

00:03:35.895 --> 00:03:39.498
And so that's a very
powerful way to count

00:03:39.498 --> 00:03:41.967
how many people have
actually been infected,

00:03:41.967 --> 00:03:44.570
whether they recognize
the symptoms or not.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:45.538
Narrator: These tests
can measure

00:03:45.538 --> 00:03:47.473
how many antibodies
are in the sample

00:03:47.473 --> 00:03:50.409
by looking at how they block
or respond to the virus.

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:52.178
These measurements 
can help to understand

00:03:52.178 --> 00:03:55.247
immunity levels and how
long immunity could last.

00:03:55.247 --> 00:03:58.084
For example, studies for
many other viral infections

00:03:58.084 --> 00:03:58.684
have found that

00:03:58.684 --> 00:04:00.119
the more severe the case,

00:04:00.119 --> 00:04:01.320
the longer the immunity.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.289
Basically, the bigger
the infection,

00:04:03.289 --> 00:04:04.724
the bigger the 
immune response

00:04:04.724 --> 00:04:06.726
and more 
antibodies in a sample,

00:04:06.726 --> 00:04:08.794
which in turn gives
longer immunity.

00:04:08.794 --> 00:04:11.530
But this may not hold
true for COVID-19.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.866
Crotty: It certainly
may not be as simple as

00:04:13.866 --> 00:04:15.768
if you're positive
for the antibody,

00:04:15.768 --> 00:04:18.437
you're protected against
the disease, you're immune.

00:04:18.437 --> 00:04:20.940
That's true for
many infections;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.010
it's not proven for
COVID-19 disease.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:26.512
Narrator: And the 
effectiveness of certain

00:04:26.512 --> 00:04:29.615
serology tests for
COVID-19 has been mixed.

00:04:29.615 --> 00:04:31.484
Some tests are
being misused,

00:04:31.484 --> 00:04:33.419
and others were brought
into the United States

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:35.354
before the FDA could
approve them.

00:04:35.354 --> 00:04:37.790
The result has been
poor detection rates,

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.592
some as low as 20%.

00:04:39.592 --> 00:04:41.193
Crotty: You know, these
are the same types of tests

00:04:41.193 --> 00:04:43.229
as a pregnancy test.

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:45.064
And so there's no way people
would take pregnancy tests

00:04:45.064 --> 00:04:47.366
if they were only
accurate 60% of the time.

00:04:47.366 --> 00:04:48.501
Narrator: And there 
have been instances

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:51.937
of false positives, which
can be extremely dangerous,

00:04:51.937 --> 00:04:53.139
because they 
arm people with

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:55.474
a false sense of
potential immunity.

00:04:55.474 --> 00:04:57.143
But these problems
aren't universal,

00:04:57.143 --> 00:04:59.879
and the FDA has begun
approving a select few

00:04:59.879 --> 00:05:01.847
that show much
higher accuracy.

00:05:01.847 --> 00:05:04.150
So, with an accurate
serology test,

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.551
would you be immune?

00:05:05.551 --> 00:05:06.886
Well, one early
Chinese study

00:05:06.886 --> 00:05:09.889
found 30% of those who
tested positive for the virus

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:12.224
had little to no 
detectable antibodies,

00:05:12.224 --> 00:05:15.427
which would suggest that
immunity isn't guaranteed,

00:05:15.427 --> 00:05:17.630
though this has been
challenged by another study

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.365
that found all
patients tested

00:05:19.365 --> 00:05:21.534
had significant 
antibody levels.

00:05:21.534 --> 00:05:24.069
But other issues 
such as age or health

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:26.005
could play into
these responses.

00:05:26.005 --> 00:05:28.641
Crotty: Is it gonna
be 0.1% of people

00:05:28.641 --> 00:05:31.443
who can get reinfected
three months later?

00:05:31.443 --> 00:05:35.147
Or is it going to be a
higher number in the elderly?

00:05:35.147 --> 00:05:36.782
Narrator: Once more 
accurate and universal

00:05:36.782 --> 00:05:38.017
testing is underway,

00:05:38.017 --> 00:05:40.619
more studies can begin
to more precisely examine

00:05:40.619 --> 00:05:43.355
how long this immunity
may last and who has it.

00:05:43.355 --> 00:05:45.858
So our best bet right now
is to keep our distance

00:05:45.858 --> 00:05:47.993
and assume we're
not immune at all.

